          }
        ],
        "sessions": [
          {
            "n": 1,
            "part": 1,
            "art": "water",
            "title": "Drawn out of the water",
            "read": "Exodus 1–2; Acts 7:17–29",
            "page": 5,
            "end": 8,
            "questionsPage": 6,
            "brief": "Israel’s family grew in Egypt until a new king arose “which knew not Joseph”. He set taskmasters over them; when the Hebrew midwives, who “feared God”, would not kill the boys at birth, Pharaoh ordered that every son born be cast into the river.",
            "briefRefs": "Exodus 1:7–22",
            "key": {
              "ref": "Exodus 2:10",
              "text": "And the child grew, and she brought him unto Pharaoh’s daughter, and he became her son. And she called his name Moses: and she said, Because I drew him out of the water."
            },
            "questions": {
              "observe": 4,
              "interpret": 2,
              "reflect": 1
            },
            "keyVerses": 3
          },
          {
            "n": 2,
            "part": 2,
            "art": "bush",
            "title": "The bush",
            "read": "Exodus 3–4; Acts 7:30–35",
            "page": 9,
            "end": 12,
            "questionsPage": 10,
            "brief": "Forty years after he fled, by Stephen’s count, Moses was keeping the flock of Jethro his father in law at Horeb, the mountain of God. The angel of the LORD appeared to him in a flame of fire out of a bush that “burned with fire, and the bush was not consumed”. When Moses turned aside to see, God called him by name, told him to put off his shoes, and named himself the God of Abraham, of Isaac and of Jacob.",
            "briefRefs": "Acts 7:29–30; Exodus 3:1–6",
            "key": {
              "ref": "Exodus 3:14",
              "text": "And God said unto Moses, I AM THAT I AM: and he said, Thus shalt thou say unto the children of Israel, I AM hath sent me unto you."
            },
            "questions": {
              "observe": 3,
              "interpret": 3,
              "reflect": 1
            },
            "keyVerses": 3
          },
          {
            "n": 3,
            "part": 3,
            "art": "door",
            "title": "Let my people go",
            "read": "Exodus 5–12",
            "page": 13,
            "end": 17,
            "questionsPage": 15,
            "brief": "Moses and Aaron told Pharaoh, “Thus saith the LORD God of Israel, Let my people go”. Pharaoh answered, “Who is the LORD, that I should obey his voice”. He stopped giving the Israelites straw for their bricks but demanded the same number of bricks, and the officers of Israel, beaten, blamed Moses and Aaron. Moses asked the Lord, “why is it that thou hast sent me?”",
            "briefRefs": "Exodus 5:1–23",
            "key": {
              "ref": "Exodus 5:1",
              "text": "And afterward Moses and Aaron went in, and told Pharaoh, Thus saith the LORD God of Israel, Let my people go, that they may hold a feast unto me in the wilderness."
            },
            "questions": {
              "observe": 4,
              "interpret": 2,
              "reflect": 1
            },
            "keyVerses": 3
          },
          {
            "n": 4,
            "part": 3,
            "art": "sea",
            "title": "The sea",
            "read": "Exodus 13:17–15:27",
            "page": 18,
            "end": 22,
            "questionsPage": 20,
            "brief": "God did not lead the people by the way of the land of the Philistines, though it was near, but “about, through the way of the wilderness of the Red sea”. Moses carried the bones of Joseph with him, and the LORD went before them in a pillar of a cloud by day and a pillar of fire by night.",
            "briefRefs": "Exodus 13:17–22",
            "key": {
              "ref": "Exodus 14:13",
              "text": "And Moses said unto the people, Fear ye not, stand still, and see the salvation of the LORD, which he will shew to you to day: for the Egyptians whom ye have seen to day, ye shall see them again no more for ever."
            },
            "questions": {
              "observe": 4,
              "interpret": 2,
              "reflect": 1
            },
            "keyVerses": 3
          },
          {
            "n": 5,
            "part": 3,
            "art": "mountain",
            "title": "Sinai",
            "read": "Exodus 19–20; Exodus 24",
            "page": 23,
            "end": 26,
            "questionsPage": 24,
            "brief": "In the third month after leaving Egypt, Israel camped before the mount in the wilderness of Sinai. From the mountain the LORD told Moses to remind the people how he “bare you on eagles’ wings”, and to say that if they obeyed his voice and kept his covenant, they would be “a kingdom of priests, and an holy nation”. The people answered together, “All that the LORD hath spoken we will do.”",
            "briefRefs": "Exodus 19:1–8",
            "key": {
              "ref": "Exodus 19:4",
              "text": "Ye have seen what I did unto the Egyptians, and how I bare you on eagles’ wings, and brought you unto myself."
            },
            "questions": {
              "observe": 4,
              "interpret": 1,
              "reflect": 2
            },
            "keyVerses": 3
          },
          {
            "n": 6,
            "part": 3,
            "art": "tablets",
            "title": "The calf and the glory",
            "read": "Exodus 32–34",
            "page": 27,
            "end": 31,
            "questionsPage": 29,
            "brief": "While Moses was in the mount, the people told Aaron, “Up, make us gods, which shall go before us”. Aaron made a molten calf from their golden earrings, built an altar before it and proclaimed a feast. The LORD told Moses that the people had corrupted themselves, that he would consume them and make of Moses a great nation. Moses besought him, reminding him of Egypt and of his oath to Abraham, Isaac and Israel, and the LORD repented of the evil.",
            "briefRefs": "Exodus 32:1–14",
            "key": {
              "ref": "Exodus 32:32",
              "text": "Yet now, if thou wilt forgive their sin—; and if not, blot me, I pray thee, out of thy book which thou hast written."
            },
            "questions": {
              "observe": 4,
              "interpret": 2,
              "reflect": 1
            },
            "keyVerses": 3
          },
          {
            "n": 7,
            "part": 3,
            "art": "grapes",
            "title": "Forty years",
            "read": "Numbers 13–14; Numbers 20",
            "page": 32,
            "end": 36,
            "questionsPage": 34,
            "brief": "At the LORD’s word Moses sent twelve men, a ruler from every tribe, to search the land of Canaan. After forty days they came back to Kadesh with a cluster of grapes carried between two on a staff, and said that the land “floweth with milk and honey”, but that the people were strong and the cities walled. Caleb said, “Let us go up at once, and possess it”; the others brought an evil report: “we were in our own sight as grasshoppers”.",
            "briefRefs": "Numbers 13:1–33",
            "key": {
              "ref": "Numbers 14:9",
              "text": "Only rebel not ye against the LORD, neither fear ye the people of the land; for they are bread for us: their defence is departed from them, and the LORD is with us: fear them not."
            },
            "questions": {
              "observe": 4,
              "interpret": 2,
              "reflect": 1
            },
            "keyVerses": 3
          },
          {
            "n": 8,
            "part": 3,
            "art": "nebo",
            "title": "Nebo",
            "read": "Deuteronomy 31–34; Deuteronomy 3:23–28",
            "page": 37,
            "end": 40,
            "questionsPage": 39,
            "brief": "At a hundred and twenty years old Moses told Israel that the LORD had said he would not go over Jordan, and that Joshua would go over before them. In the sight of all Israel he told Joshua, “Be strong and of a good courage”; the LORD gave Joshua the same charge at the tabernacle.",
            "briefRefs": "Deuteronomy 31:1–8; Deuteronomy 31:14; Deuteronomy 31:23",
            "key": {
              "ref": "Deuteronomy 31:8",
              "text": "And the LORD, he it is that doth go before thee; he will be with thee, he will not fail thee, neither forsake thee: fear not, neither be dismayed."
            },
            "questions": {
              "observe": 3,
              "interpret": 3,
              "reflect": 1
            },
            "keyVerses": 3
          }
        ],
